ne/connect/_dlt.py — C1 no-phone-home).

NOTE (NE-046 SQL connector, ne/connect/sql): sqlalchemy and psycopg are already
pinned+hash-verified in the workspace uv.lock via sibling packages (7 packages
already depend on sqlalchemy; psycopg rides the DBOS Transact dependency), so
NE-046 adds no new lock entry for either. dlt's core `sql_database` source
(reached only via the ne/connect/_dlt chokepoint) is dlt-core — no new dlt extra.
psycopg (LGPL-3.0-only) is already owner-approved with an explicit case-by-case
override in tools/supply-chain/license-policy.toml (used unmodified, dynamically
imported as a SQLAlchemy dialect — the LGPL dynamic-linking exception applies).

pyodbc (MIT, zero Python dependencies) is the ONE new lock entry this unit adds —
C4-verified 2026-07-06 (no CISA-KEV entries, actively maintained, no telemetry).
It is used exclusively as the MSSQL SQLAlchemy dialect driver
(`mssql+pyodbc://...`, ne/connect/sql/engine.py). Live MSSQL connections
additionally require the SYSTEM Microsoft ODBC Driver 18 for SQL Server
(`msodbcsql18`, Microsoft EULA) AND a system ODBC driver manager (`unixODBC` on
Linux/macOS, `iODBC` also supported) — pyodbc's compiled extension dynamically
links against the driver manager's shared library even to be IMPORTED, so both
are deployment/dev-environment prerequisites, never Python dependencies, and
never enter uv.lock or the C4 license gate. pymssql (LGPL-2.1) was evaluated
during planning and NOT adopted — pyodbc's MIT license strictly dominates under
C4 with no case-by-case approval needed.

